"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[4689],{84689:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-11","slug":"base-11","trackId":"base","order":12,"title":"10 Mistakes: How to Avoid Losing Money","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":9,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-08"],"markdown":"Lesson 11 \xb7 5 min\\n\\n## 10 Mistakes\\n\\nHow to avoid losing money\\n\\nDuring this course, we have explored cryptocurrencies, wallets, stablecoins, DeFi, loans, buying cryptocurrency and trading. Now let us gather the most important ideas in one place. Losing money in crypto does not require becoming the victim of a sophisticated hack. The cause is usually much simpler:\\n\\nfailing to check; failing to understand; rushing; believing too strongly in your own idea.\\n\\nBelow are ten mistakes that beginners should be especially careful to avoid.\\n\\n### 1. Breaking Basic Security Rules\\n\\nFollowing a link from Telegram. Entering a seed phrase on an unknown website. Approving an operation you do not understand. Downloading a suspicious file.\\n\\nTrusting someone who contacts you first and claims to be support. A scammer often has no need to hack the blockchain. It is much easier to make you grant access to your money yourself. The rule is simple:\\n\\ntake your time, verify the website and the person, read what you approve, and never give your seed phrase to anyone.\\n\\n### 2. Creating One Point Whose Compromise Loses Everything\\n\\nOne wallet. One computer. One key. If compromising one device is enough to access all your money, your storage system is too vulnerable.\\n\\nA cold wallet alone does not solve every problem either. For significant amounts, arrange storage so that one mistake or one compromised device is not enough to lose all your capital. That is why separate wallets, separate devices and multisignature arrangements exist.\\n\\n### 3. Making a Transfer Error\\n\\nA confirmed cryptocurrency transaction generally cannot simply be reversed. Before sending a transfer, always check:\\n- which asset you are sending;\\n- which network you are using;\\n- which address you are sending to;\\n- whether the recipient supports that network.\\n\\nThe same USDT can exist on Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab), Tron, Solana ↗ (opens in a new tab) and other networks. When sending a large amount to a new address for the first time, make a small test transfer first.\\n\\nAn extra fee costs less than losing the entire amount through an error.\\n\\n### 4. Buying at the Cheapest Price Without Checking the Source\\n\\nOne seller offers USDT for 100 rubles. Another offers it for 99. Choosing the second seems obvious. But the amount of crypto you receive is not the only consideration: where it came from matters too.\\n\\nIf funds are linked to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky sources, problems may appear later, for example when you transfer them to a major exchange. Check the platform and counterparty, consider AML risks and retain purchase records.\\n\\nSaving 1% is not worth the risk of receiving problematic funds.\\n\\n### 5. Failing to Check Whether the Wallet Can Be Recovered\\n\\nPeople usually think about protecting a wallet against theft. But there is another risk:\\n\\nlosing access to your money yourself.\\n\\nA phone can be lost. A computer can break. An app can be deleted. Simply writing down a seed phrase and forgetting about it is not enough.\\n\\nBefore sending a significant amount to a new wallet, make sure the backup is stored correctly and that you understand how to restore access. It is safer to test this with an empty wallet or a wallet holding a small amount than to experiment with your main storage. A simple rule:\\n\\na backup is useful only if it can actually restore access.\\n\\n### 6. Using DeFi Without Understanding Where the Yield Comes From\\n\\nYou see:\\n\\n15% annually.\\n\\nNext to it is a large button:\\n\\nDeposit.\\n\\nEverything looks simple. But before investing money, you need to understand:\\n- who pays the yield;\\n- where it comes from;\\n- which asset it is paid in;\\n- what risks you accept.\\n\\nYield can come from loan interest, fees, network rewards or distributions of new tokens. These are entirely different mechanisms. One of the course\'s central rules is therefore:\\n\\nif you do not understand where the yield comes from, you do not understand the risk you are taking.\\n\\n### 7. Taking a Loan Without Understanding Liquidation Risk\\n\\nSuppose you own $10 000 worth of ETH. You provide it as collateral and borrow 3 000 USDC. You now have both an asset whose price changes and a debt that must be repaid. If ETH falls sharply, the system can automatically sell part of the collateral to cover the debt.\\n\\nThis is called liquidation. The maximum available loan is therefore not the same as a safe loan. You must always understand what will happen to your collateral during a severe market decline.\\n\\n### 8. Not Knowing Why You Need Cryptocurrency at All\\n\\nUSDT. Then Bitcoin. Then a new token. Then DeFi.\\n\\nThen futures. After a few months, you have made many transactions but still have no answer to the main question:\\n\\nwhat am I trying to achieve?\\n\\nPreserve savings? Access dollar-denominated value? Invest? Earn income?\\n\\nTrade? These are different tasks that need different tools. Before any serious decision, it helps to answer:\\n\\nWhat do I want to achieve?\\n\\nWhat risk am I willing to accept?\\n\\nUnder what conditions will I admit that my plan has failed?\\n\\nWithout a plan, decisions quickly become driven by fear, greed and the market\'s latest move.\\n\\n### 9. Treating Trading as an Easy Way to Make Money\\n\\nYou can open an exchange account in a few minutes. You can open a trade in a few seconds. Neither teaches you professional trading. A beginner enters a market where the other participants include traders with years of experience, large companies and automated trading systems.\\n\\nThe analogy is the same:\\n\\nyou have walked into a boxing gym for the first time and are already entering a professional ring.\\n\\nLeverage is especially dangerous: it does not improve your ability, but magnifies the consequences of a mistake. Trading is therefore built around more than constantly guessing prices. It requires:\\n- a system;\\n- risk limits;\\n- statistics;\\n- discipline;\\n- psychological resilience.\\n\\n### 10. Failing to Keep a Financial History\\n\\nAfter a few years, it can be hard to remember:\\n\\nwhere you bought cryptocurrency; how much it cost; which fees you paid; where the funds came from.\\n\\nA bank, exchange, tax authority or you yourself may need this information to calculate your actual result. Keep:\\n- purchase and sale histories;\\n- exchange statements;\\n- bank transfer records;\\n- fee records;\\n- documents from exchange services;\\n- the history of cryptocurrency movements.\\n\\nYou should always understand:\\n\\nhow much you have invested, what you bought, where it is held and which documents substantiate the transactions.\\n\\n### What Almost All These Mistakes Have in Common\\n\\nThe mistakes differ. You might send cryptocurrency on the wrong network. You might trust a scammer. You might take an oversized loan.\\n\\nYou might chase a high yield. But the cause is usually the same:\\n\\nsomeone acts before understanding the consequences.\\n\\nBefore a serious transaction, ask yourself four questions:\\n\\nWhat am I doing right now?\\n\\nWhy am I doing it?\\n\\nWhat could go wrong?\\n\\nHow much could I lose if I am wrong?\\n\\nIf you have no answer, it may be better to hold off on the transaction.\\n\\nBefore a serious transaction: can you answer every question? If you cannot answer “What am I doing right now?”, it is better to wait.\\n\\n### The Main Point\\n\\nAfter this course, you do not need to know absolutely everything about cryptocurrency. But you should already understand when an ordinary action creates additional risk. Check what you do. Do not use tools you do not understand.\\n\\nThink in advance about what happens if you make a mistake. And remember:\\n\\nmissing a potential profit is almost always better than losing money in an instrument you do not understand.\\n\\nOne last question remains. If earning consistently from trading is so difficult, who profits from the people who enter crypto seeking easy money?\\n\\nIn the final lesson, we will examine how crypto influencers and people who sell forecasts, signals and courses make money. ← Lesson 10 Exchanges and Trading \xb7 Lesson 12 → Forecast Sellers\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Liquidation** — The forced sale of collateral after its value falls. In crypto, code simply executes the condition: nobody calls or warns you first\\n- **Stablecoin** — A cryptocurrency pegged one-to-one to the dollar (USDT, USDC). A “dollar flash drive”: transfer it to another country in a minute. More money passes through stablecoins than through Bitcoin\\n- **Cold Wallet** — Personal crypto storage where only you hold the private key. This is how large amounts are stored, rather than on an exchange\\n- **DeFi (Decentralized Finance)** — An attempt to rebuild the banking system with smart contracts: no banks, offices or employees, only code. It operates 24/7, but without insurance. Both a revolution and a casino\\n- **Ethereum** — Not a currency but a platform, a “world computer,” running smart contracts, DeFi and most stablecoins. Second-largest in size and trust\\n- **USDC** — Circle’s stablecoin, considered the most transparent: regular audits and clearly described reserves\\n- **USDT (Tether)** — The largest stablecoin, supporting almost all crypto turnover. For Russians after 2022, a practical tool for cross-border transfers outside banks\\n- **Yield** — The annual percentage an investment actually earns at today’s price. A bond’s price and yield move in opposite directions: when the bond becomes cheaper, its yield rises. Government bond yields influence loan and deposit rates\\n- **Futures Contract** — An agreement to buy or sell something in the future at a price agreed today. Futures prices show market expectations, from oil prices to Federal Reserve interest-rate decisions. These are expectations, not forecasts; the market is regularly wrong\\n- **Seed Phrase** — 12 or 24 words used to restore a wallet. Whoever has the phrase controls the money, so neither support staff nor project administrators need to ask for it\\n- **Multisignature (multisig)** — A wallet requiring signatures from multiple devices for a transfer, for example 2 of 2 or 2 of 3. Stealing one key is still not enough to withdraw the money\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n- **AML Check** — A review of a crypto address’s history for links to fraud, hacks and sanctions. Exchanges perform these checks themselves; they reduce risk but provide no guarantee\\n- **Leverage** — A position larger than your own funds, financed with borrowing: at 10x leverage, $1 000 opens a $10 000 position. Price movements affect your capital 10 times more strongly, in either direction\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-11-1","prompt":"Which 4 questions should you ask before any serious cryptocurrency transaction involving money?","options":[{"id":"base-11-1-1","label":"‘Which token will rise?’, ‘Where can I get leverage?’, ‘What does the blogger say?’, ‘When should I sell at the peak?’","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. ‘What am I doing right now?’, ‘Why am I doing it?’, ‘What could go wrong?’, ‘How much will I lose if I am wrong?’"},{"id":"base-11-1-2","label":"‘What am I doing right now?’, ‘Why am I doing it?’, ‘What could go wrong?’, ‘How much will I lose if I am wrong?’","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. ‘What am I doing right now?’, ‘Why am I doing it?’, ‘What could go wrong?’, ‘How much will I lose if I am wrong?’"},{"id":"base-11-1-3","label":"‘Which network is cheapest?’, ‘Who offers a bonus?’, ‘How can I bypass escrow?’, ‘Where is APY highest?’","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. ‘What am I doing right now?’, ‘Why am I doing it?’, ‘What could go wrong?’, ‘How much will I lose if I am wrong?’"}],"explanation":"‘What am I doing right now?’, ‘Why am I doing it?’, ‘What could go wrong?’, ‘How much will I lose if I am wrong?’","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2435–2450, 2975–2976"},{"id":"q-base-11-2","prompt":"What does the mistake ‘Creating one point whose compromise loses everything’ mean?","options":[{"id":"base-11-2-1","label":"Keeping all funds on one exchange or in one hot wallet on a work computer without separating risks","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Keeping all funds on one exchange or in one hot wallet on a work computer without separating risks"},{"id":"base-11-2-2","label":"Using different passwords for different services","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Keeping all funds on one exchange or in one hot wallet on a work computer without separating risks"},{"id":"base-11-2-3","label":"Writing a seed phrase on paper","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Keeping all funds on one exchange or in one hot wallet on a work computer without separating risks"}],"explanation":"Keeping all funds on one exchange or in one hot wallet on a work computer without separating risks","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2312–2317"},{"id":"q-base-11-3","prompt":"Why should you test wallet recovery before transferring large amounts to it?","options":[{"id":"base-11-3-1","label":"To make sure the recorded seed phrase really restores access to that address without errors","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. To make sure the recorded seed phrase really restores access to that address without errors"},{"id":"base-11-3-2","label":"To speed up block confirmations on the network","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To make sure the recorded seed phrase really restores access to that address without errors"},{"id":"base-11-3-3","label":"To receive a discount on gas","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To make sure the recorded seed phrase really restores access to that address without errors"}],"explanation":"To make sure the recorded seed phrase really restores access to that address without errors","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2338–2349"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":2287,"lineEnd":2482}}')}}]);